"Monitors an exponentially weighted process statistic with uncertainty limits designed to detect gradual or small sustained shifts.\n\nUse when: Recent and earlier observations should contribute with exponentially decreasing weights. A stable baseline supports a defined small-shift monitoring design.\n\nAvoid when: An arbitrary smoother would be mistaken for a statistically calibrated control chart.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes observation order. Vertical position encodes the recursive EWMA statistic, with baseline-dependent control limits around its target.\n\nBuild: Choose a smoothing weight, initialization, and baseline variation estimate. Compute each EWMA as a weighted combination of the new value and previous EWMA. Draw the statistic with correctly calculated startup or steady-state limits and a documented signal rule.\n\nCheck: Check the smoothing weight lies within its valid range. Verify the limit formula matches initialization, time index, and sampling assumptions. State whether the statistic uses individual values or subgroup means.\n\nPitfalls: A smaller smoothing weight increases memory and changes detection behavior, not just appearance. Serial dependence changes false-alarm behavior and requires an appropriate monitoring design rather than unchanged textbook limits.\n\nExample: Monitor synthetic sensor drift with a declared EWMA weight and visibly widening startup limits derived from a stable baseline.\n\nAlternatives: CUSUM chart, Individuals control chart, Line chart."
